This directory builds ONE shared libgnu.a for every GNU package in
sys/src/ape/cmd that needs gnulib.

We have tried to reduce the dependency on gnulib as much as possible by
re-implementing a lot of the API using thin shims in libap. Some things
are bigger and weirder and those things are probably better to keep in a
separate library -- that is what this is.


How it is put together
----------------------

Sources come from sys/src/external/gnulib, a single consolidated tree
produced by that directory's import.sh from the gnulib copies vendored
inside the individual GNU packages. Priority is by gnulib vintage,
newest first:

    coreutils (2026) > m4 (2026) > diffutils (2025) > ggrep (2025)
    > patch (2025) > gtar (2023) > gsed (2022) > bison (2021)

coreutils is the base -- it has the newest gnulib and the widest module
coverage -- so it supplies config.h and decides the header set that
everything compiles against. Later trees only fill in modules coreutils
does not ship (bitset, fstrcmp, timevar, spawn-pipe and friends).

This replaced an arrangement where each package compiled its own gnulib
objects and appended them to a shared archive with 'ar vu'. Which copy of
a module ended up in the archive then depended on build order, and stale
members outlived the lists that put them there.


Two rules for anyone editing the OFILES list
--------------------------------------------

1. Never add a module that libap already provides.

   libap.a and libgnu.a are both on the link line for every package, so a
   module present in both is a duplicate-symbol error, not a silent
   preference. The failure can be indirect: gnulib splits obstack into
   obstack.c and obstack_printf.c, while libap's string/obstack.c
   implements both halves in one object. Linking gnulib's obstack.o
   satisfied _obstack_begin, but the first reference to obstack_printf
   then dragged in libap's object, which redefined every _obstack_*
   symbol. Symptom:

       _obstack_begin: libap.a(obstack_printf): redefinition: _obstack_begin

   Already provided by libap, do not add: obstack, exitfail, and the
   whole x*alloc family -- xmalloc, xalloc-die, xreallocarray, xnmalloc,
   xnrealloc, xcharalloc, xzalloc, x2realloc, x2nrealloc, xpalloc and the
   idx_t variants (ximalloc, xirealloc, xicalloc, xizalloc, ximemdup,
   ximemdup0).

2. Do not let a package rebuild a module that lives here.

   The same duplicate-symbol trap applies to a package's own private
   archive. diff's libdiff.a and sed's libsed.a existed only to hold
   version-etc and progname, which this library now supplies, so both
   were removed outright.

The mkfile deletes libgnu.a before re-archiving rather than using
'ar vu' in place, so dropping a module from OFILES actually removes it.
